 ],
 "literal": "Without the bull's leg touching, threshed by young men / of meager yield millet, the poor-looking heap / to the bound claimants given, the remnant / hungry bards having eaten, finally because of running out / kinsfolk's poverty to remove, in his own village / to petty-minded people, words spoken to their faces having spoken / even for millet who begs, the noble man / if the king comes, is a capable man who will withstand!",
 "translation": "The meager yield of millet was threshed by young men without using bulls. From that poor-looking heap, after giving the share due to the tax collectors, the remnant was eaten by hungry bards. When it finally ran out, to dispel the poverty of his kinsfolk, this noble man went to the petty-minded people of his own village, spoke pleadingly to their faces, and begged for millet. Yet, if an enemy king comes with his army, he is a mighty warrior capable of standing strong and resisting them!",
 "modern": "காளைகளின் கால்கள் மிதிக்காமல் இளைஞர்களே கைகளால் அடித்து உதிர்த்த, குறைந்த விளைச்சலைத் தந்த வரகின் பொலிவற்ற குவியலிலிருந்து, முறைப்படி செலுத்த வேண்டிய கடன்காரர்களுக்குக் கொடுத்தது போக எஞ்சியதை, பசியோடு வந்த பாணர்கள் உண்டு தீர்த்தனர். அதுவும் தீர்ந்துபோன நிலையில், தன் சுற்றத்தாரின் வறுமையைப் போக்குவதற்காக, தன் ஊரிலுள்ள உலோபிகளான சிறு மனிதர்களின் முகத்திற்கு நேராக நின்று இரந்து கேட்டு வரகு வாங்கும் இந்த வள்ளல், பகை வேந்தன் தன் படையோடு வந்தால் எதிர்த்து நின்று தாங்கும் வல்லமை படைத்த வீரன் ஆவான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai and the theme of Mūtiṉ Mullai, which celebrates the ancient valor of a warrior's household. The poet describes a noble warrior who is so impoverished that his meager millet harvest had to be threshed by hand without bulls, and even that small amount was quickly exhausted after paying dues and feeding hungry bards. To save his family from starvation, this proud man is forced to beg for millet from the petty, miserly people of his own village. Yet, the poem highlights his dual nature: though he is reduced to begging in times of peace, he becomes an unstoppable force of defense if an enemy king attacks. The contrast between his humble domestic poverty and his supreme military capability beautifully illustrates the heroic ideals of classical Tamil society.",
